## Q1 "SimRacer automated framework testing process-level races Yu Srisa-an Rothermel ISSTA 2013" (standard)
# snippet source pages listed: scholars.uky.edu (STVR record), archive.cps-vo.org/node/36387, springerprofessional.de/doi/10.1145/2483760.2483771
"SIMRACER first computes potential races based on runtime traces obtained by running existing tests on target processes, and then it controls process scheduling relative to the potential races so that real races can be created."
"We implemented SIMRACER on a commercial virtual platform that is widely used to support hardware/software co-design."
"They evaluated its effectiveness on sixteen real-world applications containing known process-level races."
# STVR (SIMEXPLORER) abstract snippets:
"SIMEXPLORER first uses dynamic analysis techniques to observe system execution, identify program locations of interest, and report faults related to oracles. Next, it uses virtualization to achieve the fine-grained controllability needed to exercise event interleavings that are likely to expose races."
"They evaluated the effectiveness of SIMEXPLORER on 24 real-world applications containing both known and unknown process-level race conditions."

## Q2 "\"RRF\" race reproduction framework debugging process-level races Rattanasuksun ISSRE 2016" (standard)
# snippet source: archive.cps-vo.org/node/30361 (abstract), ieeexplore.ieee.org/document/7774517 (listed in Q5)
paraphrased by the tool (no verbatim quote returned): RRF "combines existing static program analysis tools, dynamic kernel event reporting tools, and yield points" to provide "observability and controllability needed to reproduce races". No tracer/kernel/platform named.

## Q3 "SimEvo testing evolving multi-process software systems Tingting Yu ICSME 2017" (standard)
# snippet source: https://par.nsf.gov/servlets/purl/10089644 (NSF Public Access Repository copy of the paper)
"SimEvo employs novel impact analysis techniques to identify system-level concurrent events that are affected by the changes."
"It then reuses existing test cases, as well as generating new test cases, focused on the set of impacted events."

## Q4 "SimRacer process-level races implemented on Simics virtual platform ..." (extended)
# snippet source: https://par.nsf.gov/servlets/purl/10089644 (SimEvo)
"SimEvo employs the Simics Virtualization Platform [14] to observe system execution and to deterministically control occurrences of system-level events."
"SimEvo leverages the VM's controllability to permute process interleavings by causing the kernel scheduler to explore the affected interleavings."
"SimEvo was more effective than SimRacer in terms of coverage and fault detection effectiveness"
"active testing [41], [57], which first identifies anomalous event pairs and then randomly explores interleaving schedules"
# tool summary (not a quote): SimEvo evaluation covered 13 unique multi-process Linux applications.

## Q5 "SimExplorer STVR 2017 ... Simics implementation Linux kernel version" (extended)
# snippet sources: onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/am-pdf/10.1002/stvr.1634 (author manuscript), digitalcommons.unl.edu/computerscidiss/77 (T. Yu PhD dissertation, UNL 2014, "SimExplorer: A Testing Framework to Detect Elusive Software Faults")
"By using Simics, our framework also operates at the binary level so it is capable of detecting races between applications written in different languages."
"To evaluate our SimExplorer framework we conducted an empirical study using 24 Unix programs that contain actual process-level races."
dissertation (paraphrase by tool): instantiation "that tests for races at the process level, in both user and kernel modes, involving files, shared memory, hardware components, signals, and processing cores."
